WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.840
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Today, I’m going to show&nbsp;
you how certain Greeks gods directly correlate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.440
with certain Hindu gods. Now, the reason&nbsp;
why they do this is because, in both cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.200
the pantheons trace their roots back to the&nbsp;
original Indo-European religion. As you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.800
see from our Timeline of World History chart,&nbsp;
some Indo-Europeans eventually migrated west,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.080
into Europe, with one of the earliest groups&nbsp;
being the one that settled in Greece. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.720
they ended up worshipping gods like&nbsp;
Zeus and Poseidon. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.760
other Indo-Europeans migrated east,&nbsp;
eventually settling in Iran as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.520
on the Indian subcontinent. There, they ended&nbsp;
up worshipping gods such as Indra and Varuna.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.960
Another way for me explain this is by showing&nbsp;
you our World Religions Family Tree chart. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.760
you can see Indo-European polytheism as one of&nbsp;
the earliest known forms of religion. In Iran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.760
it developed into Zoroastrianism and in Europe,&nbsp;
it developed into the various types of European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.960
paganism that you’re likely familiar with,&nbsp;
such as Celtic paganism, Germanic paganism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.880
and of course Greek paganism (which is&nbsp;
closely related to Roman paganism). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.000
if we go back to Indo-European polytheism,&nbsp;
you can also see that, in South Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.200
it developed into Vedic Religion, which was one of&nbsp;
the precursors of what we today called Hinduism.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.400
For the rest of the video however I’ll mostly&nbsp;
be using our Greek Mythology Family Tree chart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.800
It covers the Primordial gods (which include many&nbsp;
famous monsters), the old gods (which are known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.320
as the Titans) and then finally the most famous&nbsp;
Greek gods of all: the new gods (or Olympians),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.640
led of course by Zeus. In this category, we not&nbsp;
only get several children of the Olympians but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.640
also several demi-gods, who were the result of&nbsp;
Olympian gods (mostly Zeus) mating with mortal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.240
women. Finally, as you can see from this key,&nbsp;
the chart is also color-coded so that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.200
immediately tell the sky gods apart from&nbsp;
the sea gods and earth gods, and so on.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.680
Now, before I continue, I’d to point out that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.680
if you want to get a copy of this chart&nbsp;
as a poster (or any of our charts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.400
such as the Timeline of World History or&nbsp;
World Religions Family Tree), just click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:23.920
the link in the description or pinned comment&nbsp;
or visit us anytime over at UsefulCharts.com.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.480
So, in Greek mythology, the family tree of&nbsp;
gods basically starts with Gaia (who is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.920
personification of the earth) and Uranus (the&nbsp;
personification of the sky). Gaia is female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:58.080
and Uranus is male so basically you have an&nbsp;
Earth Mother and a Sky Father. Which tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.120
to make sense to most people because to this&nbsp;
day, Westerners still tend to think of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.480
earth as being “Mother Earth” but when they think&nbsp;
about God (especially in the Christian sense),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.680
they tend to think of him as being male&nbsp;
and existing somewhere up in the sky.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.560
These associations predate Christianity, as&nbsp;
evidenced by these two primordial Greek gods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.480
and in fact, they can be traced all the way&nbsp;
back to the original Indo-Europeans who had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:33.440
pair of gods called Dyḗus (who was male) and&nbsp;
Dʰéǵʰōm (who was female). In their language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:40.400
those two words literally meant “The Daylight&nbsp;
Sky” and “The Earth”. Now lest you think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.720
all humans just tend to think of the God up in the&nbsp;
sky as being male and the Earth as being female,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.560
well, let me tell you: that's simply not&nbsp;
true. For example, in Ancient Egypt, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:00.400
actually the opposite. Geb, who was male, was the&nbsp;
Earth, whereas Nut, who was female, was the sky.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.320
The only reason that European cultures tend to&nbsp;
associate the sky with a male god and the earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.320
with a mother is because of the fact that European&nbsp;
culture can be traced back to Indo-European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:19.040
culture. And the same is true of Indian culture.&nbsp;
In the Vedic pantheon (and hence in Hinduism),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:26.640
there is a god called Dyaus (which, again, means&nbsp;
sky) and a goddess called Prithvi (which, yup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:33.920
you guessed it — means Earth). Now, obviously&nbsp;
the name Dyaus comes directly from Dyḗus but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.120
why then do we get Prithvi instead&nbsp;
of something close to Dʰéǵʰōm? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.560
it’s actually quite simple. Dʰéǵʰōm was&nbsp;
also called something like Pl̥th₂éwih₂&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.360
(which means “the broad one”) so Prithvi is&nbsp;
simply based on the alternative name. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.040
so where do the names Uranus and Gaia come from&nbsp;
then? Well, the name Uranus is connected to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:03.680
original Indo-European word for rain (something&nbsp;
that comes from sky) and Gaia, well, the thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.880
there is that the people living in Greece&nbsp;
before the Greeks arrived already had a Mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.960
Earth goddess named Gaia so when the newcomers&nbsp;
arrived, they simply said, “Well, obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:22.080
Gaia is Dheghom so let’s just call her Gaia now&nbsp;
since that appears to be the local name for her”.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.920
So, at the end of the day, it is obvious that,&nbsp;
in Greek mythology, Uranus and Gaia come directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:35.920
from Dyḗus and Dʰéǵʰōm; and in Hinduism, it’s&nbsp;
obvious that Dyaus and Prithvi come directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.960
from them as well. So, that’s the first major&nbsp;
connection between Greek gods and Hindu gods.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:49.760
What else is there? Well, in Greek Mythology, we&nbsp;
next get a set of old gods (called the Titans),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.640
followed by a set of new gods (called the&nbsp;
Olympians). Interestingly, none of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:02.160
Titans (meaning these 12 here) connect directly to&nbsp;
the original Indo-European gods but several of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:08.800
main Olympians do. It used to be thought that&nbsp;
the whole Olympians replacing the Titans story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:15.600
was based on the Indo-European gods replacing&nbsp;
earlier local gods but this is no longer the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.160
The thinking nowadays is that the story was&nbsp;
more about dynastic succession and/or memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:28.240
of cataclysmic events such as earthquakes.&nbsp;
But either way, gods like Cronus and Rhea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.440
were almost certainly pre-Indo-European&nbsp;
gods, worshipped long before the Greeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:41.360
arrived. This is why Cronus stands out as being&nbsp;
not a sky god, whereas both Uranus and Zeus are.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.200
There are however three gods from the Titan&nbsp;
generation that do connect directly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:54.960
Indo-European gods and thus to Hindu gods as well.&nbsp;
These are Helios (the God of the Sun), Selene (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:02.000
Goddess of the Moon), and Eos (the Goddess of the&nbsp;
Dawn). In the original Indo-European pantheon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:10.960
they were known as Seh₂ul, Meh₁not, and H₂éwsōs.&nbsp;
In Roman mythology, Helios is known as Sol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:18.000
(so the linguistic connection to Seh₂ul is quite&nbsp;
clear). And while it’s not mentioned on the chart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:26.320
Selene was also known as Mene (from whence we get&nbsp;
the words month and menstruation) so there you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.240
can see that there’s a linguistic connection&nbsp;
to Meh₁not. Finally, the name Eos definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:38.960
comes directly from H₂éwsōs. In terms of Hindu&nbsp;
gods, their god of the sun is called Surya (so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:45.520
again, that’s linguistically related to Seh₂ul.&nbsp;
The Hindu god of the moon is Chandra. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.720
there’s no direct linguistic connection&nbsp;
to Meh₁not but there is a connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:57.120
the early Indo-European word kand, meaning “to&nbsp;
shine” (which was likely used when referring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:04.080
to Meh₁not). Take note that kand is also the&nbsp;
origin of the English word candle. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:11.840
the Hindu goddess of the dawn is Ushas (a name&nbsp;
that once again comes directly from H₂éwsōs.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:18.960
Okay, let’s now move on to the Olympians. In this&nbsp;
generation, we find Zeus and his two brothers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.280
Poseidon and Hades. Zeus is the God of&nbsp;
the Sky, Poseidon the God of the Sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:31.840
and Hades the God of the Underworld. When it comes&nbsp;
to these three very important Greek gods, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:42.400
three have direct counterparts in Hinduism. Zeus&nbsp;
is Indra, Poseidon is Varuna, and Hades is Yama.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:48.400
Let’s look at the Zeus-Indra connection first.&nbsp;
As I’m sure you know, in ancient Greece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:54.800
Zeus was considered to be the king of the gods.&nbsp;
Well, during the Vedic period in Ancient India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.440
it was Indra who was the king of the gods. But&nbsp;
the parallels don’t stop there. As I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:08.160
Zeus was a sky god (in fact the word Zeus&nbsp;
almost certainly comes from the word Dyḗus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.960
which we saw earlier, meant sky). Well, Indra&nbsp;
was a sky god too. And not only were Zeus and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:21.760
Indra both sky gods, they were both—more&nbsp;
specifically—thunder gods, responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:27.520
controlling the weather, having the ability&nbsp;
to create storms when they were angry. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.480
why are Zeus and Indra so similar? Well,&nbsp;
again, it’s because they were both based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:39.520
on an earlier Indo-European god. In this case,&nbsp;
the name of that original god was Perkʷūnos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:47.520
Perkʷūnos was a later version of Dyḗus and was, in&nbsp;
some places, seen as being the son of Dyḗus. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:54.160
while Perkʷūnos eventually became known as Zeus&nbsp;
in Greece and Indra in India, his original name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:01.920
is still evident in Slavic mythology, where the&nbsp;
chief god and god of thunder is known as Perun.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:09.520
Okay, let’s now look at Poseidon and Varuna.&nbsp;
Both are very clearly gods of the sea and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:15.760
water in general, but etymologically, both&nbsp;
of their names actually go back to Uranus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:24.160
who, as we saw earlier, was a sky god. This is&nbsp;
more clear with Varuna because Varuna and Uranus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:30.320
sound a bit similar. But the link is there with&nbsp;
Poseidon too. You see, Poseidon originally meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:36.560
“husband of the earth”. And who was the husband&nbsp;
of the earth? Well, Uranus, of course. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:44.560
the thinking is that, originally, Uranus (aka&nbsp;
Poseidon / Varuna) was one and the same as Dyeus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:50.640
the main sky god, husband of the earth—but,&nbsp;
over time, he was sort of spun off to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:58.960
a separate god of the sea instead, eventually&nbsp;
becoming Poseidon in Greece and Varuna in India.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:05.680
What about Hades and Yama? They too have a bunch&nbsp;
of similarities. For example, both are gods of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:12.080
the underworld, meaning the place where people go&nbsp;
after they die. Both have strange looking guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:20.880
dogs. Hades has Cerberus (who has three heads)&nbsp;
whereas Yama has Sharvara and Shyama (two dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:26.640
each of which has four eyes). On top of&nbsp;
this, both gods are associated with rivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.680
which are said to mark the boundary between&nbsp;
this world and the underworld. For Hades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.880
that river is Styx, whereas for&nbsp;
Yama, it’s the river Vaitarani.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:43.840
Now, Zeus, Poseidon and Hades also had three&nbsp;
sisters: Demeter, Hera, and Hestia. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:49.520
the connections to earlier Indo-European&nbsp;
gods and hence to Hindu gods is far weaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:56.480
and in one case non-existent. The name Demeter&nbsp;
means “Earth Mother” and therefore she may be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.680
newer version of Dʰéǵʰōm, the original&nbsp;
Mother Earth goddess. Hera though is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:07.840
definitely not an Indo-European god. Like&nbsp;
Rhea (who has a similar-sounding name),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:13.920
she was likely adopted from the Minoan&nbsp;
civilization on Crete. Finally, there’s Hestia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:20.000
whose name means “hearth” or “fireplace”. She&nbsp;
may be connected to the early Indo-European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:27.280
god H₁n̥gʷnis, who was the god of fire, but&nbsp;
that’s a bit of stretch. In Hinduism however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:35.200
there is a Vedic god of fire called Agni&nbsp;
and he most certainly is based H₁n̥gʷnis.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.960
Okay, there is just one more connection&nbsp;
between Greek gods and Hindu gods to mention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:46.720
While they are not shown on this chart, there&nbsp;
are a set of twin gods in Greek mythology called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.280
Castor and Pollux. If you’re familiar with the&nbsp;
constellation or star sign Gemini, that’s who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:59.840
I’m talking about. Well, Hinduism also a set of&nbsp;
twin gods known as the Ashvins. In both cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:06.080
the sets of twins are associated with horses,&nbsp;
with the transitions between night and day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:10.320
and with healing or rescuing. And of&nbsp;
course the reason for this is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:16.560
there was probably an original pair of twin&nbsp;
gods worshipped by the early Indo-Europeans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:22.560
that both Castor and Pollux as well as the&nbsp;
Ashvins are based upon. Unfortunately though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:29.200
in this case, scholars have not been&nbsp;
able to reconstruct their original names.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.520
Okay, so that was a look at some of&nbsp;
the connections between Greek gods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:40.960
and Hindu gods. Of course, also connected&nbsp;
are Norse gods, Celtic gods, Slavic gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:47.040
early Iranian gods, and so on. I wasn’t able&nbsp;
to point out all of those correlations in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.600
video but if you know of any links&nbsp;
that are particularly interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:58.240
do let us know in the comments. And if you want to&nbsp;
grab your own copy of this Greek Mythology poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:05.440
or any of the other charts that we sell, do&nbsp;
check us out over at UsefulCharts.com. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:10.000
dropped a link to our shop in both&nbsp;
